---
# The search index, built at deploy time. No plugin, so the native GitHub Pages
# build produces it exactly as `jekyll serve` does.
#
# Posts and pages both, because a reader looking for the advisories table or
# the about page is searching the site, not a corpus of write-ups. Pages carry
# no date and no tags, so each entry says which kind it is and the renderer
# lays the two out differently.
#
# Bodies are truncated: the index is downloaded in full by anyone who opens
# /search/, and a handful of long write-ups would otherwise make that a
# multi-megabyte fetch. 8000 characters covers roughly the first two thirds of
# a median-length post; the title, description and tags carry the rest of the weight.
layout: null
permalink: /search.json
sitemap: false
---
{%- comment -%}
  A comma before every entry except the first, rather than `forloop.last`.
  Entries are filtered inside the loop, so the last iteration is not
  necessarily the last entry emitted — and one trailing comma is the whole
  file failing to parse.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign first = true -%}
[
{%- for post in site.posts -%}
{%- unless first %},{% endunless -%}{%- assign first = false -%}
{
"kind": "post",
"title": {{ post.title | jsonify }},
"url": {{ post.url | relative_url | jsonify }},
"date": {{ post.date | date: site.date_format | jsonify }},
"iso": {{ post.date | date_to_xmlschema | jsonify }},
"tags": {{ post.tags | jsonify }},
"description": {{ post.description | default: post.excerpt | strip_html | normalize_whitespace | truncate: 220 | jsonify }},
"body": {{ post.content | strip_html | normalize_whitespace | truncate: 8000 | jsonify }}
}
{%- endfor -%}

{%- comment -%}
  Every page that a reader can land on, minus three that would be noise:
  /404.html, which is what you get when you were looking for something else;
  /search/ itself; and everything under /research/. Anything else opts out with
  `search: false` in its front matter. The URL test keeps the feed, the
  sitemap, opensearch.xml and this file out — they are pages to Jekyll but not
  to a reader.

  /research/ is excluded here, in a tracked file, rather than left to the
  .gitignore that keeps it off GitHub. The folders under it hold proof-of-
  concept material for work that is not published yet, and a rule that lives
  only in .gitignore is one deleted line away from putting their titles in an
  index the whole site downloads. Two independent reasons for them to be
  absent is the right number. Drop this when the write-ups are out and the
  folder is tracked alongside them.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- for p in site.pages -%}
{%- assign ext = p.url | split: "." | last -%}
{%- assign head = p.url | slice: 0, 10 -%}
{%- if p.url == "/404.html" or p.url == "/search/" or head == "/research/" or p.search == false -%}
  {%- continue -%}
{%- endif -%}
{%- unless p.url contains ".html" or ext == p.url or p.url == "/" -%}
  {%- continue -%}
{%- endunless -%}
{%- unless first %},{% endunless -%}{%- assign first = false -%}
{
"kind": "page",
"title": {{ p.title | default: "Untitled" | jsonify }},
"url": {{ p.url | relative_url | jsonify }},
"tags": [],
"description": {{ p.description | strip_html | normalize_whitespace | truncate: 220 | jsonify }},
"body": {{ p.content | strip_html | normalize_whitespace | truncate: 8000 | jsonify }}
}
{%- endfor -%}
]
